# STOFS-3D Atlantic post-processing

This README records how the post-processing workflow changes SCHISM outputs.
Each output-file family should have its own section so inputs, mutations, and
derived products can be traced independently.

## Version notes

The following scripts should have been updated with v7.2:

- `extract_slab_fcst_netcdf4.py`
- `generate_adcirc.py` has a bug fix that has not yet been incorporated into
  the operational forecast.

## `Shapefiles/city_poly.node_id.txt`

### Current v7.2 product

The city-node mask was regenerated on 2026-08-28 using node coordinates from:

```text
/sciclone/schism10/feiye/STOFS3D-v8/v7.2_static_inputs_2025_06_20/hgrid.gr3
```

That path is a symbolic link which currently resolves to:

```text
/sciclone/schism10/feiye/STOFS3D-v8/I15w_v7/hgrid_levee_2025_xgeoid20b.gr3
```

The mesh has 5,872,610 elements and 3,052,121 nodes. Its SHA-256 checksum at
generation time was
`4f844719bfbe238a825a61850ab935b5541653413bbaade642d19e479ebc16b0`.

The polygon input was `Shapefiles/city_poly.shp`, which contains 71 features
in WGS 84 longitude/latitude coordinates. The mask was calculated with the
same operational rule used by `generate_adcirc.py`: read each feature with
pyshp, pass its point coordinates to
`matplotlib.path.Path.contains_points()`, and take the union of the selected
nodes across all features.

The output is a plain-text Boolean mask, not a list of node IDs. Line 1 maps to
mesh node 1, and each subsequent line maps to the corresponding 1-based mesh
node. Its validated contents are:

| Property | Value |
| --- | ---: |
| Total lines | 3,052,121 |
| Non-city values (`0`) | 2,992,030 |
| City values (`1`) | 60,091 |
| Other values | 0 |
| File size | 6,104,242 bytes |
| SHA-256 | `73ae134663cca2d7f167215f9074ac3c11eed0efe44331da1390249be38fec8a` |

Sanity checks performed during generation included sequential node-ID and
finite-coordinate validation of the mesh, a reload of the written text file,
and an independent OGR reading of the polygons. The pyshp and OGR masks had
zero differing nodes. Feature 26 is the only polygon with an interior ring; a
ring-aware containment check also had zero differences for this mesh.

OGR reports features 1, 2, 12, and 13 as invalid because each collapses to a
two-point, zero-area segment. They were retained during generation so the
result continues to match the operational pyshp/matplotlib behavior. Replace
or repair these source features before changing the mask algorithm to a strict
geometry-validating implementation.

## `out2d_*.nc`

### Summary

| Script | Input or target | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `filter_isolated_ponds.py` | Files matching `*out2d_*.nc`, or a selected `out2d_<id>.nc` | Modifies each matched file in place by adding or replacing `isolatedPondNode`. It reads `dryFlagNode` but does not change `dryFlagNode` or `elevation`. |
| `mask_boundary_elev.py` | `hgrid.ll` and `Shapefiles/additional_masks.shp` | Does not modify `out2d`. It creates the auxiliary `lbnd_node_mask.nc` used by `mask_lbn_nd.sh`. |
| `../MaskLargeElevation/mask_lbn_nd.sh` | Currently hard-coded to `out2d_1.nc` | Appends `idmask` to the raw input file in place, then creates the derived file `out2d_1.mask.nc`, in which masked `elevation` values are set to `-99999`. |

The archived `TEMP/filter_isolated_ponds.py_ver_2026-0707` has the same
in-place `isolatedPondNode` behavior if it is run manually.

### Isolated-pond mask

`filter_isolated_ponds.py` reads `dryFlagNode` at every time step and identifies
wet nodes that are not connected to a wet seed node. The result is stored in
the original `out2d` file as:

- Variable: `isolatedPondNode`
- Dimensions: `(time, nSCHISM_hgrid_node)`
- Type: signed byte (`i1`)
- Values: `0` for a node that is not an isolated pond and `1` for an isolated
  pond node

The file is opened in NetCDF append mode. If `isolatedPondNode` already exists,
its values are replaced; otherwise, the variable and its attributes are added.
`gen_geojson.py` reads this variable and applies it as an additional elevation
mask. Other `out2d` consumers currently ignore it.

### Land-boundary/high-ground mask

#### Generating `lbnd_node_mask.nc`

Run `mask_boundary_elev.py` from a working directory containing:

- `hgrid.ll` (a runtime/staged input; it is not stored in this directory)
- the complete `Shapefiles/additional_masks` shapefile set (`.shp`, `.shx`,
  `.dbf`, `.prj`, and `.cpg`), which is stored in this directory

From that prepared working directory, run:

```shell
python mask_boundary_elev.py
```

The current additional-mask shapefile contains 15 polygon features in
EPSG:4326.

The script reads `hgrid.ll`, computes grid boundaries and node connectivity,
and forms the union of three node masks:

1. Land-boundary neighborhood: all land-boundary nodes, sides touching those
   nodes, elements adjacent to those sides, and every node in those elements.
2. High ground: nodes satisfying `hgrid.ll` depth `dp < -19.0`.
3. Additional polygons: nodes selected by
   `Shapefiles/additional_masks.shp`.

It writes `lbnd_node_mask.nc` in NetCDF4 format with:

- Dimension: `nSCHISM_hgrid_node`
- Variable: `idmask(nSCHISM_hgrid_node)`
- Type: NetCDF integer
- Values: `0` for unmasked nodes and `1` for nodes selected by any of the three
  masks
- Attribute: `idmask:long_name = "Node mask"`

The same run also writes `hgrid_masked.gr3` as a diagnostic representation of
the mask.

#### Applying `lbnd_node_mask.nc`

`../MaskLargeElevation/mask_lbn_nd.sh` performs three operations:

1. `ncks -A` appends `idmask` from `lbnd_node_mask.nc` directly to
   `out2d_1.nc`. This changes the raw file.
2. `ncap2` copies the augmented input to `out2d_1.mask.nc` and sets
   `elevation=-99999.0` wherever `idmask == 1`.
3. `ncatted` sets `elevation:missing_value` to `-99999.0` on the derived file.

Despite the older `../MaskLargeElevation/README` referring to `out2d_*.nc`, the
current shell script processes only `out2d_1.nc` unless its `infile` and
`outfile` assignments are changed or the script is wrapped in a loop.

## Adding another output-file family

Add a new section at the same level as `out2d_*.nc` and record:

1. Raw inputs and generated outputs.
2. The scripts and commands involved, in execution order.
3. Whether each input is read-only, modified in place, or copied to a derived
   product.
4. Variables, dimensions, attributes, fill values, and masks that are added or
   changed.
5. Required grids, shapefiles, caches, and other auxiliary inputs.
